 have two B5 as a potential pair, and several tiles that could develop into sequences (W3, T3, T5,T6,T7). However, passing means missing an immediate meld that progresses toward a winning shape.\\n\\n**Reason:**  \\nChi T7 is highly attractive because it immediately forms a third meld, leaving only 3 tiles in hand. Since B5 pair is already present, you just need one more meld (either Chow or Pung) from W3 or T3. The unshown tiles show W3 (1 left), T3 (1 left), T5 (3 left), and B5 (4 left) are available. This accelerates your hand toward a ready (\\u542c\\u724c) state. Also, having three exposed Chows leans toward **All Chows** (2 points) plus possibly **Mixed Shifted Chows** if sequences are in different suits and shifted. Your current chows: W3\\u2013W5, T2\\u2013T4, and the new T5\\u2013T7 are consecutive across suits (W, T, T) \\u2013 not quite a mixed shifted set (requires different suits each step), but still helps.  \\n\\nPassing retains flexibility but delays progress. Given you already have two exposed melds, hiding more tiles isn\\u2019t crucial; the hand is not aiming for a concealed-only pattern. The discard after Chi will be tricky (likely W3 or T3), but with B5 as pair candidate, discarding the odd tile W3 or T3 reduces suit variety, moving toward a simpler wait.\\n\\n**Answer:** Chi T7\"}", "tile": "{\"system_prompt\": \"\\n    You are a Chinese Standard Mahjong (\\u56fd\\u6807\\u9ebb\\u5c06, or MCR Mahjong) player. It is a four-player game, and it is played with a basic set of 136 tiles with Chinese characters and symbols.\\n    Players draw and discard tiles in turn until they complete a winning hand with a 14th tile. The basic type of winning hand consists of four melds and a pair, while there exist winning hands with several special patterns.\\n    **Tiles and Representation**\\n    * There are 4 identical copies of each suited tile and honored tiles.\\n    * Tiles are represented using Letter+Number, i.e. W7, T2, B4, F1, J3, etc.  \\n    * Suited 